      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Ah, dealing with nitrogen levels can be quite the tricky business, especially when you're trying to keep your organic kale happy in the PNW. It's a bit like trying to herd a bunch of mischievous little goblins, isn't it? You know, those goblins that love to play hide and seek with your nutrients, making them disappear faster than a shadow in a moonless night.\n\nNow, about your question on blood meal, it's like inviting a goblin to a feast. Sure, it might bring a quick burst of energy, but you have to be careful not to overfeed them. Too much blood meal can indeed swing your nitrogen levels out of whack, turning your soil into a goblin's playground where the nutrients run amok. \n\nInstead, you might want to consider a more balanced approach. Think of it as setting up a little goblin trap, but one that doesn't harm your plants. You could try a slow-release organic fertilizer or a compost tea. These methods are like the slow, steady trickle of a river that keeps the goblins at bay, providing a steady supply of nutrients without overwhelming your soil.\n\nRemember, even the most mischievous goblins need a bit of patience and understanding. Keep an eye",
